Developers are questioning whether a settlement of the U.S. government's <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/07/17/apple-doj-antitrust-settlement/">antitrust case</a> against Apple would meaningfully loosen the company's control over the iPhone, according to <a href="https://www.theinformation.com/articles/apples-antitrust-settlement-talks-spur-worries-among-developers"><em>The Information</em></a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2024/03/Apple-vs-DOJ-Feature.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-943221" /><br/>
<br/>
The report highlights PayPal and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/epic-games/">Epic Games</a> among the companies that have raised concerns with the Department of Justice (DOJ). Officials have reportedly indicated that they favor a negotiated resolution and have asked developers to suggest improvements to how the iPhone works with other companies' products.<br/>
<br/>
These discussions largely center on interoperability, including access for competing accessories and payment services. The concern among developers and antitrust lawyers cited in the report is how much Apple would actually have to change. Rival smartwatches, for example, lack much of the integration available to the Apple Watch, while competing payment apps reportedly still face disadvantages despite gaining access to near-field communication (NFC) technology for contactless payments.<br/>
<br/>
When the DOJ <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2024/03/21/apple-sued-by-us-department-of-justice/">filed its lawsuit in March 2024</a>, it alleged that restrictions affecting messaging, digital wallets, smartwatches, cloud gaming, and multipurpose apps helped Apple unlawfully protect its smartphone monopoly. Apple rejected these allegations and argued that the government's proposed intervention would threaten its ability to design integrated products.<br/>
<br/>
Some of the capabilities at issue have changed since the lawsuit began. Apple added Rich Communication Services (<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/rcs/">RCS</a>) support to Messages with iOS 18, improving communication with Android phones. It also <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2024/08/14/apple-opening-up-iphones-nfc-chip-to-developers/">expanded access to the iPhone's NFC capabilities</a> for third-party contactless transactions starting with iOS 18.1.<br/>
<br/>
